1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,229
Welcome to Wind Procure, a pilot for Great Britain's day-ahead electricity procurement team.

2
00:00:04,229 --> 00:00:06,181
The product starts with two questions.

3
00:00:06,181 --> 00:00:10,410
What wind forecast could the team actually have seen when making its decision?

4
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:12,688
And how wrong has that forecast been?

5
00:00:12,688 --> 00:00:17,567
This dashboard brings those answers together, with an explicit decision cutoff and an audit trail.

6
00:00:17,567 --> 00:00:23,423
The pilot covers decisions made in January twenty twenty-six, using only Elexon's wind forecasts and wind generation outturn.

7
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:25,701
The first part presents the customer experience.

8
00:00:25,701 --> 00:00:28,629
The second explains the implementation for a technical reviewer.

9
00:00:29,279 --> 00:00:35,611
Here we are replaying the decision on the fourteenth of January, at nine fifty G M T, for delivery on the fifteenth.

10
00:00:35,611 --> 00:00:42,231
The wind forecast averages eleven point six eight gigawatts over its native target points, with a peak of fifteen point eight three gigawatts.

11
00:00:42,231 --> 00:00:45,685
The solid line shows the forecast that was eligible at the cutoff.

12
00:00:45,685 --> 00:00:48,851
The dashed line shows the actual outturn, available afterwards for evaluation.

13
00:00:48,851 --> 00:00:52,018
The daily mean absolute error is one point zero nine gigawatts.

14
00:00:52,018 --> 00:00:55,472
These actuals are hindsight, clearly separated from information used for the decision.

15
00:00:56,122 --> 00:00:59,345
Changing the cutoff to nine twenty makes the point-in-time rule tangible.

16
00:00:59,345 --> 00:01:04,033
The nine thirty publication is now too late, so the app selects the earlier eligible forecast.

17
00:01:04,033 --> 00:01:06,670
The mean changes to eleven point six one gigawatts.

18
00:01:06,670 --> 00:01:12,824
A chart based on today's latest revision would miss that distinction and could make historical performance look better than it was.

19
00:01:12,824 --> 00:01:18,391
Wind Procure selects a forecast separately for every delivery target, using only publications at or before the chosen cutoff.

20
00:01:18,391 --> 00:01:23,080
The available presets cover N two E X, E P E X and an early review.

21
00:01:23,730 --> 00:01:27,005
The forecast explorer shows exactly what was published and when.

22
00:01:27,005 --> 00:01:30,607
For the nine fifty decision, the nine thirty issue is selected.

23
00:01:30,607 --> 00:01:34,210
Later issues remain visible for auditing, but are marked as excluded.

24
00:01:34,210 --> 00:01:38,140
The settlement table also preserves each target's publication time and lead time.

25
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:44,035
There is an important coverage detail: the retrieved forecast series has hourly targets, while actual outturn is half-hourly.

26
00:01:44,035 --> 00:01:46,328
We compare exact matching start times only.

27
00:01:46,328 --> 00:01:47,965
Missing half-hour forecasts stay missing.

28
00:01:47,965 --> 00:01:53,533
The monthly evaluation therefore has seven hundred and forty-four matched points, rather than an invented full profile.

29
00:01:54,183 --> 00:01:58,499
At the nine fifty cutoff, the January mean absolute error is about two gigawatts.

30
00:01:58,499 --> 00:02:02,507
Root mean square error is two point five gigawatts, so larger misses matter.

31
00:02:02,507 --> 00:02:07,747
The positive bias of one point seven three gigawatts means the forecast tended to exceed the outturn.

32
00:02:07,747 --> 00:02:10,214
Over eighty-four percent of matched targets overforecast wind.

33
00:02:10,214 --> 00:02:13,296
Weighted absolute percentage error is seventeen point six eight percent.

34
00:02:13,296 --> 00:02:15,763
The daily bars help identify days worth reviewing.

35
00:02:15,763 --> 00:02:24,086
These figures describe the difference between the requested Elexon series; metering scope and curtailment may also contribute, so this is not a pure attribution of forecast skill.

36
00:02:24,736 --> 00:02:27,705
The decision brief adds an empirical wind shortfall scenario.

37
00:02:27,705 --> 00:02:33,973
It uses up to fourteen earlier completed delivery days, and only actual records published by the historical decision cutoff.

38
00:02:33,973 --> 00:02:36,282
At least forty-eight matched points are required.

39
00:02:36,282 --> 00:02:43,540
For the fourteenth of January, the historical ninetieth-percentile shortfall is four point six gigawatts, based on two hundred and eighty-eight earlier points.

40
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:46,509
Early January deliberately shows insufficient history, as seen here.

41
00:02:46,509 --> 00:02:50,468
This scenario is not a calibrated confidence interval or a purchase instruction.

42
00:02:50,468 --> 00:02:54,426
It makes uncertainty visible without suggesting more certainty than the data support.

43
00:02:55,076 --> 00:03:02,665
The team can export a decision brief, the forty-eight-period settlement C S V, or a J S O N evidence bundle with source provenance.

44
00:03:02,665 --> 00:03:06,144
The methodology page explains every assumption and links the original datasets.

45
00:03:06,144 --> 00:03:13,100
The result is a shared basis for a procurement conversation: a known forecast, a quantified historical error, and a traceable decision cutoff.

46
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:17,527
This pilot does not know the client's demand, contracted wind share or other resources.

47
00:03:17,527 --> 00:03:20,057
A portfolio-specific purchase quantity needs those additional inputs.

48
00:03:20,057 --> 00:03:25,432
The value here is a transparent national wind signal that a trading team can inspect and challenge.

49
00:03:26,082 --> 00:03:28,131
Now for the internal technical review.

50
00:03:28,131 --> 00:03:29,839
The architecture has three boundaries.

51
00:03:29,839 --> 00:03:33,596
An offline Python ingestion command retrieves and validates the Elexon records.

52
00:03:33,596 --> 00:03:38,719
A deterministic analysis layer indexes revisions, selects admissible forecasts, joins exact timestamps and calculates metrics.

53
00:03:38,719 --> 00:03:43,159
Fast A P I exposes the results to a React and TypeScript frontend.

54
00:03:43,159 --> 00:03:47,940
The fixed archive is small enough that a database is unnecessary for this pilot.

55
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:52,722
The production request path makes no upstream network call and writes no persistent state.

56
00:03:52,722 --> 00:03:56,820
This keeps the historical replay reproducible and suitable for a serverless deployment.

57
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:04,846
In backend slash ingest dot py, forecast requests filter publication windows, while actual requests filter settlement dates and wind fuel type.

58
00:04:04,846 --> 00:04:10,114
Forecast context begins on the thirty-first of December; actual context includes the first of January.

59
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:13,275
The decision evaluation itself stays strictly within January decisions.

60
00:04:13,275 --> 00:04:21,704
There are sixty-four archived daily source requests, eighteen thousand six hundred and eighty-eight forecast revisions, and one thousand five hundred and thirty-six wind actuals.

61
00:04:21,704 --> 00:04:23,460
Bounded retries handle transient failures.

62
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:26,973
Identical duplicate keys are collapsed; conflicting equal-publication values fail validation.

63
00:04:26,973 --> 00:04:31,890
Every response has a retrieval timestamp and a S H A two fifty-six hash.

64
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:35,979
The key implementation is in backend slash analysis dot py.

65
00:04:35,979 --> 00:04:38,731
Each target's revisions are sorted by publication time.

66
00:04:38,731 --> 00:04:43,546
A binary search chooses the latest publication less than or equal to the cutoff.

67
00:04:43,546 --> 00:04:47,674
Selection is per target, rather than assuming that one file contains everything.

68
00:04:47,674 --> 00:04:50,426
A forty-eight-period delivery grid preserves every actual half-hour.

69
00:04:50,426 --> 00:04:56,961
Forecasts and actuals join only on identical target timestamps; missing values remain null, and zero is a valid observation.

70
00:04:56,961 --> 00:04:59,369
Error is forecast minus actual in megawatts.

71
00:04:59,369 --> 00:05:04,184
The frontend converts to gigawatts only for presentation, and never recalculates the statistical results.

72
00:05:04,834 --> 00:05:06,861
There are two distinct actual-data views.

73
00:05:06,861 --> 00:05:08,888
Retrospective evaluation uses the archived outturn.

74
00:05:08,888 --> 00:05:15,307
Decision-time risk restricts actual revisions to those published by the historical current cutoff, and also excludes incomplete delivery days.

75
00:05:15,307 --> 00:05:18,685
Earlier forecasts are selected using their own original decision cutoff.

76
00:05:18,685 --> 00:05:24,428
This avoids a subtler form of leakage in which future actuals train an apparently historical risk estimate.

77
00:05:24,428 --> 00:05:30,846
The tests verify that adding a later forecast or a future actual revision cannot change the selected decision-time risk.

78
00:05:30,846 --> 00:05:36,252
The empirical quantile is deliberately simple, with its sample count and limitations exposed to the user.

79
00:05:36,902 --> 00:05:40,583
The H T T P layer is in backend slash app dot py.

80
00:05:40,583 --> 00:05:44,830
It validates January decision dates and twenty-four-hour cutoff strings, returning four twenty-two for invalid inputs.

81
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:49,361
Day, month, metadata, C S V and health endpoints are documented through Fast A P I.

82
00:05:49,361 --> 00:05:55,308
On the frontend, src slash data dot t s owns abortable requests and exports; the chart components are S V G.

83
00:05:55,308 --> 00:06:00,405
A static Q A preview can load results generated by the same Python code, and is explicitly labeled.

84
00:06:00,405 --> 00:06:04,369
The public Vercel app is confirmed to use the real Python A P I.

85
00:06:05,019 --> 00:06:07,897
The verification suite focuses on the analytical contract.

86
00:06:07,897 --> 00:06:16,171
Fifteen backend tests passed, covering cutoff equality, future revision exclusion, per-target selection, missing data, valid zeros, metric arithmetic and the February delivery boundary.

87
00:06:16,171 --> 00:06:20,129
They also compare the Python-generated preview with the A P I.

88
00:06:20,129 --> 00:06:22,287
TypeScript and the production build passed.

89
00:06:22,287 --> 00:06:26,964
Anonymous requests to the live deployment returned the application and Python endpoints successfully.

90
00:06:26,964 --> 00:06:34,159
Browser checks covered cutoff changes, publication history, filters, an empty search, date boundaries and a real C S V download.

91
00:06:34,159 --> 00:06:38,476
The source package records both the checks performed and the environment limits.

92
00:06:39,126 --> 00:06:46,227
For local reproduction, Docker Compose builds the React frontend in a Node stage, then runs the Python application as a non-root user.

93
00:06:46,227 --> 00:06:50,100
The container honors the port environment variable and has a health check.

94
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:54,619
Without Docker, npm run dev colon full starts both development services after dependency installation.

95
00:06:54,619 --> 00:07:02,366
Docker itself was not available in this execution environment, so its local build is not claimed as verified; a continuous-integration smoke test is supplied.

96
00:07:02,366 --> 00:07:07,854
Production is deployed on Vercel, with the compressed archive bundled alongside the Python function and static frontend.

97
00:07:07,854 --> 00:07:11,081
No secrets or new Elexon request are needed at runtime.

98
00:07:11,731 --> 00:07:20,013
The R E A D M E maps every directory, explains startup and deployment, and explicitly discloses Chat G P T and Codex assistance.

99
00:07:20,013 --> 00:07:23,809
This demonstration uses a generic synthesized narrator, not the candidate's voice.

100
00:07:23,809 --> 00:07:27,259
The technical design and analysis notes make knowledge transfer straightforward.

101
00:07:27,259 --> 00:07:34,160
For a production phase, first reconcile the forecast and outturn coverage, then add authorized portfolio demand, contracts and allocation inputs.

102
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:36,921
A longer approved history would support stronger calibration.

103
00:07:36,921 --> 00:07:45,202
This first version delivers a usable, publicly accessible portal and a reproducible analysis, with each claim tied to the evidence the pilot actually has.
